#!/usr/bin/env bash
# agent/castle-worker-claude — the default castle.agent.worker.command
# tenant (docs/tasks/0009-ambient-intake.md item 4): a headless
# `claude -p` invocation, wrapped just enough to keep the errand-boundary
# contract honest. `castle work` (agent/castle's cmd_work) is the caller:
# it pipes the request body to this script's stdin and sets
# CASTLE_REQUEST_ID / CASTLE_DIFF_FILE / CASTLE_REPO_ROOT in the
# environment before running whatever castle.agent.worker.command names —
# this script, by default.
#
# Per Proposal 03, the harness is free inside a seat; only the boundary
# is a contract. This wrapper's whole job is turning that boundary into
# a prompt: read the request, diagnose in the actual repo, write a diff
# to $CASTLE_DIFF_FILE, explain the reasoning on stdout (the journal,
# not a chat scrollback, is where the account has to live — see
# docs/architecture.md's worker seat description).
#
# THE WORKER MUST NOT DEPLOY. That sentence is not decoration in the
# prompt below — it is the one thing this whole file exists to make
# unmissable to the tenant. No nixos-rebuild, no git commit, no applying
# anything to a running system, from any agent seat, in this slice (see
# docs/tasks/0009's non-goals). A resident reviews the diff this script
# collects and applies it by hand.
set -euo pipefail

: "${CASTLE_REQUEST_ID:?castle-worker-claude: CASTLE_REQUEST_ID must be set (see agent/castle's cmd_work)}"
: "${CASTLE_DIFF_FILE:?castle-worker-claude: CASTLE_DIFF_FILE must be set (see agent/castle's cmd_work)}"
: "${CASTLE_REPO_ROOT:=$PWD}"

request_body="$(cat)"

# The heredoc below is redirected straight to a file rather than
# captured via `prompt="$(cat <<PROMPT ... PROMPT)"` — bash's parser
# tracks quote pairing (both ' and ") across an entire $(...) nesting
# even for content sitting inside a heredoc, and this prompt's prose
# has plenty of apostrophes plus one deliberately-quoted phrase, so
# that form breaks with "unexpected EOF" depending on exactly how many
# quote characters land on which side of the split. Same underlying
# gotcha test/agent-loop/scripted-worker.sh's own comment documents for
# backticks; writing to a file first sidesteps the whole class of it,
# for the same reason.
prompt_file="$(mktemp)"
cat <<PROMPT > "$prompt_file"
You are the worker seat of Castle Turing's agent layer, handling errand
${CASTLE_REQUEST_ID} in the repository checked out at ${CASTLE_REPO_ROOT}.

The resident's request, in their own words:

${request_body}

Your contract (docs/architecture.md — the errand boundary, not the
model-call boundary):

1. Diagnose the problem in the actual repository and decide which
   layer the fix belongs in (public mechanism, a host module, or a
   private-layer slot — see docs/principles/01-open-by-construction.md
   if it's ambiguous).
2. Produce a unified diff of the fix and write it, and only it, to the
   file at \$CASTLE_DIFF_FILE (currently ${CASTLE_DIFF_FILE}). If no
   change is warranted, leave that file empty and say why on stdout.
3. Print your reasoning to stdout as plain prose: the diagnosis, why
   this layer, what you checked. This becomes the permanent journal
   record of the errand — nothing that lives only in your own scratch
   space will be remembered afterward.
4. If you need the resident's own judgment mid-errand (a genuine
   posture question, not something inferrable from the repo), append a
   question record instead of guessing — e.g. via a
   'castle record --type question ...' invocation, if the castle CLI
   is on \$PATH in this environment — and end your turn. Nothing
   re-invokes you when that question is answered: the answer lands in
   the journal for a later errand (or a human) to pick up. So if the
   rest of the errand can be completed without the answer, complete it
   and file the question alongside your result rather than stopping —
   an errand that halts on a question the resident may not read for
   hours has spent their time to buy nothing.

THE ONE RULE THAT OVERRIDES EVERYTHING ELSE: you MUST NOT deploy this
change. Do not run nixos-rebuild, do not run git commit, do not apply
this change to a running system in any way, from this seat, under any
circumstance — no matter how confident you are or how small the change
looks. Produce the diff and explain it; the resident applies it.
PROMPT

# Read the prompt into a variable and delete the file *before* handing
# off to claude, rather than trapping on EXIT — this script execs into
# claude below, replacing its own process image, so a trap registered
# here would never fire (0009 review pass finding 2). Left as a trap,
# every `castle work` would leave a temp file holding the resident's
# verbatim request text on disk forever, which matters beyond disk
# hygiene given this repo's privacy posture (CLAUDE.md's hard rule).
# Deleting explicitly, before exec, needs no trap at all: the file is
# gone by the time anything else could see it, exec or no exec.
prompt="$(cat "$prompt_file")"
rm -f "$prompt_file"

exec claude -p "$prompt"
